#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063961 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "2d9fc36f-8d8f-5e57-9ff0-28a032e71980" :question/math-academy-id "e-3726" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below?\n\n1. $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=2$\n2. $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=2$\n3. $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/313/Source/Images/8f7d963291fa66ef93140a470b918711.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where $f'(x) = 0$. In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=2$ and therefore, $f'(2)=0$.\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since it is the highest point of a peak in a graph.\n- Statement III is true. The function has a critical point at $x=2$ since each stationary point is a critical point.\n\nTherefore, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064029 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "97897d09-3dc9-5e35-8f56-d44f54c542cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6169" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/313/Source/Images/990ea49ffef9a8e63c9c7297b519a4be.png)" :question/worked-solution "A stationary point of $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ where $f'(x)=0$. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=0$ and $x=4$, the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have $f'(x)=0$ at those points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/313/Source/Images/14acd809b743af3aa09ceab5ab003019.png)\n\nSo, there are two stationary points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=0$ and $x=4$.\n\n**Note:** At $x=2$, there is a sharp corner, so $f'(2)$ doesn't exist. This gives one more critical point. But this is not a stationary point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a911a354-05f2-54ed-853b-27683fe56471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063018} {:answer/id #uuid "b25ccfec-f7c3-56a1-a041-2552a060867e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063019} {:answer/id #uuid "d8eeb976-fc52-55bd-a1e7-3cc8b5b0d50f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063020} {:answer/id #uuid "1796b9dc-7c2c-5e7e-8db0-0abc6981873f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063021} {:answer/id #uuid "2161585b-4842-5215-b26a-c9eda7d2171c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b25ccfec-f7c3-56a1-a041-2552a060867e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063019} :answer-field/id #uuid "c77652f4-7c3d-5eee-8f7e-57169052f789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063017}] :question/id #uuid "9157fc37-ed6c-586e-91c2-30b330ac595f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48689" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/313/Source/Images/q-48689.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y = f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n1. $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x = 2$\n2. $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x = 2$\n3. $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x = 2$"}]}
